hypotheses.statement:
Define $\rho$ by solving $$\label{cbe:scalar}
 \partial_t\rho+W\cdot\nabla_X\rho
       =\nu\Delta_X\rho+g,\qquad \rho(0,X)=0.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Equation <a href="#cbe:scalar" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cbe:scalar">[cbe:scalar]</a> has a global smooth solution. Every space-time derivative is continuous in time with values in $C_0(\mathbb R^2)\cap L^1(\mathbb R^2)$, where $C_0$ denotes continuous functions tending to zero at infinity. Furthermore, $$\label{cbe:mass}
 \rho\ge0,\qquad
 \mathcal M(t):=\int_{\mathbb R^2}\rho(t,X)\,dX=\theta(t)\le1.$$
